<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Troyes</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Augustobona, Trecae</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Troyes is a city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seine">Seine</a> in the Champagne region of north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. Founded under Augustus as Augustobona, the chief town of the Tricasses, it later took their name. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was the capital of the counts of Champagne and the seat of two of the great Champagne fairs, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chretien-de-troyes">Chrétien de Troyes</a> wrote his Arthurian romances at their court. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-troyes">Treaty of Troyes</a> was signed there in 1420.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/troyes.jpg" alt="Troyes" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: KBWEi at German Wikipedia (Original text: KBWEi 21:50, 14. Okt. 2007 (CEST)) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Troyes_centre_ville1.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
